<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!-- Best Practice: Enable TreatWarningsAsErrors in production code to catch potential issues early -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
    <!-- Best Practice: Add NoWarn for XML documentation warnings if needed, rather than disabling TreatWarningsAsErrors -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\AphaBatchJobs.Core\AphaBatchJobs.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Best Practice: Align package versions with target framework (net8.0) - use 8.0.x versions for .NET 8 projects -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="8.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="8.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>


<!-- 
Key improvements made:

1. Changed TreatWarningsAsErrors from false to true - This is a best practice for production code to ensure code quality and catch potential issues during compilation.

2. Added NoWarn for CS1591 (missing XML documentation comments) - This allows treating warnings as errors while suppressing documentation warnings, which is common in application projects.

3. Downgraded Microsoft.Extensions packages from 9.0.0 to 8.0.0 - Best practice is to align dependency versions with the target framework version (net8.0). Using 9.0.0 packages with .NET 8 can lead to compatibility issues and unnecessary dependencies.

These changes improve code quality, maintainability, and ensure better compatibility with the .NET 8 runtime.
-->